You can use a loop that keeps asking for input until a valid choice is made. For example:
Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
String[] options = {"A", "B", "C"};
String choice;
do {
    System.out.println("Choose an option:");
    for (int i = 0; i < options.length; i++) {
        System.out.println((i+1) + ". " + options[i]);
    }
    choice = scanner.nextLine().toUpperCase();
} while (!Arrays.asList(options).contains(choice));
// Then process the choice
But this assumes that the user enters the exact string as in the options. If you want to allow numbers, you can map the numbers to the options:
String[] options = {"Option 1", "Option 2", "Option 3"};
String choice;
do {
    System.out.println("Choose an option:");
    for (int i = 0; i < options.length; i++) {
        System.out.println((i+1) + ". " + options[i]);
    }
    String input = scanner.nextLine();
    if (input.matches("\\d+")) {
        int index = Integer.parseInt(input) - 1;
        if (index >= 0 && index < options.length) {
            choice = options[index];
            break;
        }
    }
    System.out.println("Invalid input. Please enter a valid option.");
} while (true);
// Then process the choice
This code will accept numbers and map them to the corresponding option. If the input is not a number or is out of range, it will prompt the user again.
